 To make the circle element look more 3D, you can add some additional CSS properties such as a higher contrast gradient for the background and a chamfer box-shadow. Here's an updated version of your `.circle` class:

```css
.circle {
 fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
 filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) drop-shadow(-15px -15px 15px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75)); /* Add negative offset to create depth */
 transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
 background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc), radial-gradient(at center, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5) 25%, transparent 25%, transparent 75%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5) 75%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)); /* Add inner white radial gradient for highlight */
 box-shadow: 10px 10px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), -10px -10px 20px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5); /* Add chamfer box-shadow */
 border-radius: 70%;
}
```

This will give the circle a more pronounced 3D effect with a subtle inner glow and a chamfered shadow that creates depth perception. The new filter property uses two drop-shadows, one positive and one negative, to create depth. The background gradient now consists of both a linear and a radial gradient to achieve a more complex and layered appearance.

