```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const TemperatureConverter = () => {
  const [temperature, setTemperature] = useState(0);
  const [unit, setUnit] = useState('celsius');

  const handleChange = (e) => {
    setUnit(e.target.value);
  };

  const celsiusToFahrenheit = (cel) => (cel * 1.8) + 32;
  const fahrenheitToCelsius = (fah) => (fah - 32) * 0.5556;

  const convertedTemperature = unit === 'celsius' ? celsiusToFahrenheit(temperature) : fahrenheitToCelsius(temperature);

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="temperature">Enter temperature:</label>
      <input type="number" id="temperature" value={temperature} onChange={(e) => setTemperature(Number(e.target.value))} />
      <br />
      <label>
        Unit:
        <select value={unit} onChange={handleChange}>
          <option value="celsius">Celsius</option>
          <option value="fahrenheit">Fahrenheit</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <br />
      <p>Converted temperature: {convertedTemperature.toFixed(2)} {unit}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TemperatureConverter;
```

This component uses the `useState` hook to manage the state of the temperature and the selected unit. The `handleChange` function updates the `unit` state when the user selects a new unit. Two helper functions are defined for converting between Celsius and Fahrenheit, and the converted temperature is calculated based on the current unit.

