 Here's an example of a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` environment variable and calls the next function if it is set to "true", otherwise returns `false`. This decorator uses both helpfulness (by providing a clear solution) and verbosity (by explaining each step in detail).

```typescript
import { createDecorator, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';
import { NextFunction, Request, Response } from 'express';

export const EnvironmentCheckMiddleware = createDecorator(
  'environmentCheck',
  (target: Object, key: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) => {
    const originalMethod = descriptor.value;

    return {
      // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-shadow
      value: async function (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
        if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'true') {
          await originalMethod.apply(this, [req, res, next]);
        } else {
          res.status(403).send(false);
        }
      },
    };
  },
);

// Usage:
@EnvironmentCheckMiddleware()
async myControllerMethod(@Req() req: Request, @Res() res: Response) {}
```

This decorator named `EnvironmentCheckMiddleware` checks the `NODE_ENV` environment variable at the beginning of every decorated method. If the value is "true", it continues with the execution of the original method. Otherwise, it sends an HTTP response with status code 403 (Forbidden) and a boolean value of `false`.

You can use this decorator on any controller or route handler methods by adding the `@EnvironmentCheckMiddleware()` decorator above them.

